  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="1e19-9475-dbac-47b7" name="Norman and Breton (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #71 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). This list runs from the granting of the Duchy of Normandy to Hrolf to the loss of Sicily by its Italo Norman rulers. While the Bretons retained a traditional fighting style, skirmishing with javelins and only charging a weakened enemy, the Normans preferred a more direct approach. This was strengthened when they adopted the lance, somewhere between 1018 and 1041, although the changeover was not universally adopted until around 1080. The nature of the feudal system restricted the size of native armies, and large forces would inevitably include a significant number of mercenaries. 

ARMY COMPOSITIONS (pick one in the Army composition list):
[Norman and Breton - Bretons]
  Cavalry: at least 50%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 50%
  Characters: Up to 25% Cavalry: At least 50% Infantry: Up to 50% Normans may not outnumber Bretons. Normans are Unreliable.
[Norman and Breton - Italo-Normans]
  Cavalry: at least 33%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 50%
  Sicilians: at least 25%
  Characters: Up to 25% Cavalry: At least 33% Infantry: Up to 50% Sicilians: At least 25%
[Norman and Breton - Norman]
  Cavalry: at least 33%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 50%
  Characters: Up to 25% Cavalry: At least 33% Infantry: Up to 50% Bretons may not outnumber Normans. Bretons are Unreliable.</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
</catalogue>
